experiment: ctxfreeze
  SHORT RUN — 150000 ticks against a minimum of 900000. derived: §57. §56 found the derived visual index COLLAPSING TWICE and never
  inverting -- deg 22/24 in the first eighth, 0/24 through the fourth to
  seventh, 16/24 AGAIN in the eighth, with the rate stepping 0.0181 ->
  0.3121. The late collapse is keyed to ABSOLUTE ticks and not to the end of
  the run: the 450k session's own eighth 8 covers 394k-450k and reads deg
  1/24 while the 900k session's eighth 4 covers the same stretch and reads
  0/24. SLEEP IS RULED OUT FROM SOURCE -- the creature does not fall asleep
  until ~1.04M ticks. Two suspects remain, both read off the kernel: M1 the
  prototypes FREEZE, since lr = 1/ctx_wins_[winner] with ctx_proto_tau_ms 0
  has no floor, ctx_wins_ is a monotone float32 counter nothing decays or
  resets, the context path runs EVERY TICK in Network::step(), and by 900k
  the update rounds to zero in float32; M2 the conscience OUTGROWS the object
  signal, since cdscale = ctx_dsum_/ctx_dn_ is a lifetime mean with no decay
  and gate 2 feeds the conscience-ADJUSTED distance back into it, a positive
  feedback which is why a threshold crossing looks like a STEP. PRIMARY: the
  ratio R = margin/pen per eighth with the two sides summed SEPARATELY, so
  the run says WHICH side moved -- M2 if the denominator rose, M1 if the
  numerator fell, NEITHER if R never crosses 1, which refutes both and is a
  result. TAU300K's floor binds only after ~300k wins, so it doubles as the
  WIRING check (a DNA scalar must not re-roll the wiring) and the vacuity
  arm. The underflow share is MEASURED against the stored value after the
  write, never predicted from eps, because |x-proto| at this operating point
  is exactly what is unknown. kRead: every branch prints
  Everything below is printed for debugging and is NOT a
  measurement; this run cannot pass whatever it reads.

experiment: ctxfreeze
  the question      §57. §56 found the derived index COLLAPSING TWICE and never
                    inverting: deg 22/24 in e1, 0/24 in e4-e7, 16/24 in e8, with
                    the rate stepping 0.0181 -> 0.3121. The first collapse is
                    understood (prototypes at the origin while rate_ema_ climbs).
                    THE SECOND IS NOT, and it is keyed to ABSOLUTE ticks: the 450k
                    run's own e8 (394k-450k) reads deg 1/24 and the 900k run's e4
                    (337k-450k) reads 0/24 -- the same stretch of life, uncollapsed,
                    scored two ways.
  not sleep         SETTLED FROM SOURCE, not measured: the creature does not fall
                    asleep until ~1.04M ticks, so in a 900k session it NEVER sleeps.
                    Consolidation cannot be involved.
  the two suspects  M1 THE PROTOTYPES FREEZE. lr = 1/wins with tau 0 and no floor;
                    wins is a monotone float32 counter nothing decays or resets
                    (only gate 7 clears it, this is gate 2) and the context path
                    runs EVERY TICK in Network::step(), so by 900k lr ~ 2e-6 and
                    proto += lr*(x-proto) rounds to zero.
                    M2 THE SCALE OUTGROWS THE SIGNAL. cdscale is a lifetime mean
                    with no decay, the penalty scales with it, and gate 2 feeds the
                    ADJUSTED distance back into ctx_dsum_ -- a positive feedback,
                    which is why a threshold crossing would look like a STEP.
  the discriminator R = margin / pen, per eighth, with the two sides summed
                    SEPARATELY and never pre-divided so the run says which moved.
                    margin = mean |d_best - d_second| over RAW distances, the object
                    signal. pen = mean SPREAD (max-min) of the conscience term,
                    because a constant added to every slot cannot change an argmin.
                    M2 if R crosses 1 at e8 by the DENOMINATOR rising; M1 if the
                    NUMERATOR falls with uf ~ 1 already early; NEITHER otherwise,
                    which is a real outcome and not a failed run.
